<spell-notebook "dropdown usage">
	<header level="1">
		Dropdown
		<header>A dropdown allows a user to select a value from a series of options.</>
	</>
	<dropdown-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#values">Values</>
			<item link="#rich-data">Rich data</>
			<item link="#forms">Forms</>
			<item link="#events">Events</>
			<item link="#invoker-commands">Invoker commands</>
			<item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
			<item link="#framework-usage">Framework usage</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  items vs options, values, forms, events, invokers, keyboard, accessibility, frameworks. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			Write `<ui-dropdown>` with `<ui-item>`s inside: the options, headers (`type="header"`) and dividers
			(`type="divider"`), in order. No JavaScript to call: Fomantic's settings are attributes.

			- The look: `selection` (a form select), `search`, `multiple`, `inline`, `button` (+ `labeled` with an `icon`),
			  `floating`, `pointing`, `appearance="simple"`, `padding="compact"`, `fluid`, `scrolling`, the `length`s ...
			- An item's `value` defaults to its text; `label` overrides what the dropdown shows once it's chosen;
			  `description`, `icon`, `image` and `flag` decorate the row, and any markup inside is projected into it.
			- Slots: `trigger` (your own trigger content), `header` (content at the top of the menu), `icon` (replaces the
			  caret, or a `labeled` icon).
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Values">
		<markdown>
			`value` is the chosen value: a string, or with `multiple` a `string[]` property (the attribute is a comma / space
			list). Set it to choose from code (no event); set it to `""` (or `[]`) to clear, which shows the placeholder
			again -- Fomantic's `clear` behaviour. A form reset restores the `value` attribute (`restore defaults`).
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Rich data">
		<markdown>
			Attributes carry primitives and are reflected; the rich `options` array is a JS property (frameworks find it with
			`"options" in element`), and first paint never needs it -- slotted `<ui-item>`s or the `value` / `label`
			attributes carry what server rendering must show. Options set through the property are added after the slotted
			items. For remote data, fetch on `ui-search` and set `options`.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			// <ui-dropdown selection clearable placeholder="Pick a framework"></ui-dropdown>
			const dropdown = document.querySelector("ui-dropdown")
			dropdown.options = [
			  { value: "react", text: "React" },
			  { value: "vue", text: "Vue" },
			  { value: "solid", text: "Solid", description: "2.0" },
			  { value: "svelte", text: "Svelte", disabled: true }
			]
			dropdown.addEventListener("ui-changed", (event) => console.log(event.detail.value))
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Forms">
		<markdown>
			A dropdown is a form control: `name` submits the value (`multiple`: one entry per value), `required` validates,
			`disabled` leaves it out and `readonly` keeps it in but unchangeable. `max-selections` caps a `multiple`.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Events">
		<markdown>
			- `ui-changed` (`{ value }`): the user chose, added, removed or cleared a value.
			- `ui-opening` / `ui-closing` (`{ open }`): the menu is about to open / close; cancelable.
			- `ui-search` (`{ query }`): a `search` dropdown's query changed.
			- `ui-added` / `ui-removed` (`{ value }`): a `multiple` dropdown gained or lost a value (`ui-added` also for an
			  `allow-additions` addition).
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			const tags = document.querySelector("ui-dropdown[name=tags]")
			tags.addEventListener("ui-added", (event) => console.log("added", event.detail.value))
			tags.addEventListener("ui-search", async (event) => {
			  tags.options = await fetch(`/tags?q=${encodeURIComponent(event.detail.query)}`).then((r) => r.json())
			})
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Invoker commands">
		<markdown>
			A button with `commandfor="id"` and `command="--toggle"` (or `--show` / `--close`) opens and closes the menu with
			no script, as a user action (the cancelable `ui-opening` / `ui-closing` first), and focuses the dropdown so the arrow
			keys work. A disabled or read-only dropdown ignores commands. Live: "Invoker commands" on the Examples tab.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			- **Arrow Down** / **Arrow Up**: open the menu, then move the highlight.
			- **Home** / **End**: highlight the first / last enabled option (open).
			- **Page Down** / **Page Up**: move the highlight several rows (open).
			- **Enter**: choose the highlighted option (or the addition); opens a `search` dropdown.
			- **Space**: choose the highlighted option (not in `search`, where it types a space).
			- **Escape**: close the menu. **Tab**: close it and move on.
			- **Backspace** (`multiple` + `search`, empty query): remove the last label.
			- Any character (no `search`): type-ahead -- open and highlight the next matching option.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			The trigger is an ARIA 1.2 combobox with a listbox popup (the WAI-ARIA authoring practices pattern).

			- `role="combobox"` with `aria-expanded`, `aria-controls`, `aria-haspopup="listbox"` and `aria-activedescendant`
			  pointing at the highlighted row. Focus stays on the trigger while you move through the list.
			- The menu is `role="listbox"` (`aria-multiselectable` for `multiple`); rows are `role="option"` with
			  `aria-selected` and `aria-disabled`. Headers and dividers are presentational.
			- `aria-label` on the host names the combobox, otherwise the placeholder does. `loading`, `readonly`, `required`
			  and validation set `aria-busy`, `aria-readonly`, `aria-required` and `aria-invalid`.
			- The clear button and each `multiple` label's delete button get translated names (`Clear`, `Remove {value}`).
			- Escape and outside clicks close the menu; leaving the dropdown with Tab closes it too.
			- If the element fails to render, its native fallback is a real `<select>`
			  (`multiple` when `multiple`), built from the same `<ui-item>`s or `options`.
			  It keeps the value, the form submission, `ui-changed`, and the browser's own keyboard pattern;
			  it drops search, additions, clearable, option icons and images, and the open / close events.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Framework usage">
		<markdown>Set `options` as a property, not an attribute, and listen for `ui-changed`.</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-react">React 19</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			<ui-dropdown selection options={options} value="x" onui-changed={(e) => setValue(e.detail.value)} />
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-vue">Vue 3.5</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<!-- compilerOptions.isCustomElement = (tag) => tag.startsWith("ui-") -->
			<ui-dropdown selection :options="options" value="x" @ui-changed="(e) => (value = e.detail.value)" />
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-solid">Solid 2</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			// events and rich data are plain props:  `on-<event>={...}`, `options={...}`
			<ui-dropdown
			  selection
			  options={options}
			  value="x"
			  on-ui-changed={(e) => setValue(e.detail.value)}
			/>
			```
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
